---
import WaspLowPolyVariants from "../../../components/studies/WaspLowPolyVariants.astro";
import WaspSketchTrace from "../../../components/studies/WaspSketchTrace.astro";
import WaspStabilizerStates from "../../../components/studies/WaspStabilizerStates.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "lhi-wasp-2026-11-01");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="purpose">
		<h2 id="purpose">01 / A trainer that turns on four stabilizers</h2>
		<p>
			The LHI Wasp is a fourth, very small ship with a different job from the
			Scout, Fighter and Heavy Corvette. It is a training vessel: a craft whose
			behaviour is simple enough that a new pilot learns the game from it, and
			predictable enough that the factory can test new mechanics on it without
			fitting choices muddying the result. Those two purposes point the same
			way. Fewer variables in the hull means both a gentler first flight and a
			cleaner experiment.
		</p>
		<p>
			The sketch sets the form: a flat-nosed dart with a long leaf canopy, broad
			delta wings carrying one upright weapon-or-utility slot each, one
			universal slot straddling the stern, and four stabilizers in two angled
			pairs, hugging the nose taper and the stern taper. Read with one grid cell
			as two ship units, the slots match the production 11 × 9 fitting footprint
			and the hull comes out about 40 units long and 28 wide, against the
			Scout's 52 × 42 and the Fighter's 73 × 40.
		</p>
		<WaspSketchTrace />
		<p>
			Stabilizers are a given, not an unknown. Every ship already flies with
			one: when a turn input is released, rotation stops instead of coasting,
			and the ship never tumbles in free fall. The simulation expresses that
			rule as direct angular response without angular momentum. The Wasp simply
			carries four of them, in two pairs, because it has no side engines to turn
			with. Turning fires the stabilizers on the side that produces the turn;
			releasing fires the opposite pair to arrest the rotation, exactly as the
			one stabilizer does on the other ships. The aft universal slot is the only
			engine position, so a lone engine pushes and the stabilizers steer. Turn
			authority therefore belongs to the chassis and never changes with the
			fitted engine, which is what makes the trainer predictable.
		</p>
		<p>
			The tension this leaves is one of expression: a hull this small must show
			four stabilizers doing their work, two upright wing slots, a stern slot
			and a large canopy without becoming a loose collection of parts, both
			enlarged in the builder and at flight size beside the Scout.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>This cycle should settle the Wasp hull: how the sketched proportions,
				four stabilizers and three slots read as one small trainer in the
				builder and in flight.</strong
			>
			Stabilizer turn rate, mass and other handling numbers, any training-mode
			content, ranked eligibility and the plume of a single aft engine stay
			outside this decision. The
			<a href="/study-references/lhi-wasp-sketch.webp">original sketch</a>
			is preserved as the reference for proportions and slot placement.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration">
		<h2 id="exploration">02 / Showing four stabilizers at work</h2>
		<h3 id="stabilizer-states">02.1 / Coasting, turning, released</h3>
		<p>
			The same sketched hull, faceted in the shared Fighter palette, in the
			three situations the stabilizer rule produces. Turning left fires the
			forward-right and aft-left stabilizers; releasing fires the opposite pair
			until the rotation stops. Jets point outward from the hull, the way a
			reaction thruster pushes, so the firing pair is always the one on the far
			side of the motion. Show the footprints to check that the wing slots and
			the stern slot stay clear of every jet.
		</p>
		<WaspStabilizerStates />
		<p>
			Geometry is the 01 trace: about 40 × 28 units, canopy 14 units long,
			stabilizer bars 1.6 wide and about 6 long, slot footprints 11.5 × 8.6 and
			11.8 × 8. The bottom strip draws the three states and the production Scout
			at the real 0.65 flight drawing scale, so the Wasp appears at roughly 26
			pixels long, as it would in play.
		</p>
		<p>
			What to notice: whether the two firing bars read as a diagonal pair rather
			than two unrelated sparks, whether the released state is distinguishable
			from the turn without reading the caption, and whether the jets survive
			the flight-scale strip at all. Nothing here is simulated. The facets, jet
			shape and glow are study fixtures; the real stabilizer rule, mass and turn
			rate are untouched, and no engine is fitted in the stern slot, so push and
			steer together remain unexamined.
		</p>
		<p>
			Owner review: the comparison is useful for a different reason than its
			question. As with the other ships, the low-poly fake-3D body has to be
			drawn first; stabilizers, slots and engines are placed on it afterwards.
			The sketch's interior lines mark where the folds are expected. 02.1 stays
			as evidence of the stabilizer firing language; its fixture hull is not
			carried forward.
		</p>
		<h3 id="low-poly-bodies">02.2 / Six low-poly bodies with real relief</h3>
		<p>
			A first set of six used the sketched folds as flat tiles with a tone
			picked per plane. None of them read as a solid, let alone a spacecraft,
			and they were replaced rather than kept. The reason is construction: the
			Fighter, Scout and Cruiser get their volume from one consistent relief, a
			lit left side, a shaded right side and a spine that is actually the
			highest line. A fold only reads when the planes on each side of it face
			different ways.
		</p>
		<p>
			These six therefore give every fold node a height. Each plane's facing is
			computed from those heights and shaded from one light ahead, to the left
			and above, through the shared six-tone Fighter palette. All keep the
			sketched outline; they differ in which folds exist and in the height
			profile, which is what changes the body's character. Show the fold lines
			to compare structure; hide them to judge the body as the builder would
			show it.
		</p>
		<WaspLowPolyVariants />
		<p>
			01 and 02 keep every sketched fold and differ only in whether the spine or
			the wingtips are highest. 03 replaces the inner folds with a level deck
			and sharp chines. 04 is the Cruiser's one-apex fan. 05 raises two ridges
			along the spars and sinks the canopy between them. 06 adds the shoulder
			cross fold as a high nose blade and lifts the stern into a saddle.
		</p>
		<p>
			Chassis planes only: no stabilizers, slots, engines or stern notch, and
			the canopy is a placeholder leaf. Heights are study fixtures chosen for
			reading, not measured parts; the shading is a flat-plane lighting
			estimate, not the production renderer, which stores finished facet colours
			rather than heights. A chosen body would be converted to fixed facets at
			integration, as the Scout and Cruiser were.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / To be developed after 02</h2>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
